# Feature Delivery — Research and Design Gate (FDA-001 v1.2)

**Purpose:** Canonical rules for **deep discovery**, **product requirement inventory**, **initial product/architecture/UI-UX design**, **internet research**, **UI/UX review**, and **queued feature execution** before and during implementation.

**Principle:** Repository evidence first. No invented paths, commands, package names, or architecture. Research reduces uncertainty — it never overrides repository constraints or hard stops.

---

## 1. Deep discovery gate (CP0)

Before any source edit, the agent must inspect **read-only** evidence:

| Area | What to inspect |
| ---- | --------------- |
| Repository instructions | Root `AGENTS.md`, nested `AGENTS.md`, `.cursorrules`, `.cursor/rules/` |
| Workspace graph | `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, root `package.json` scripts, per-package `package.json` — **pnpm workspace only** (never NX / `nx affected`) |
| Routes and BFFs | App route trees, route registrars, BFF handlers, `*-http-contracts` packages |
| Domain layer | Ports, repositories, dispatchers, `*.operations.ts`, workspace bindings |
| Events / outbox | `apps/api/src/outbox/`, domain side-effects, enqueue/relay patterns |
| Data / tenant | Prisma models, migrations, RLS policies, tenant guards, `tenantId` sourcing |
| Adjacent modules | Similar behavior in other bounded contexts (grep + codebase-memory graph) |
| Tests | Unit, contract, Postgres, browser, accessibility specs for touched surfaces |
| Workspaces | `workspace.manifest.json`, capabilities, Denali / Urban / starter differences |
| Docs / standards | Phase docs, ADRs, design freezes, post-v1 exclusions |
| Runtime (when needed) | Dev env files, `AGENTS.md` Cloud instructions, health endpoints, available services |

**Rules:**

- Cite **exact paths** and **exact commands** from the repo — never invent.
- Record findings in `.cache/feature-delivery/<sessionId>/requirement-inventory.json` (discovery section).
- CP0 is **not complete** until discovery checklist rows have evidence refs.

---

## 2. Product requirement inventory

For every requested capability, classify:

| Class | Definition |
| ----- | ---------- |
| `member-interactive` | Member-visible action or state change on Portal/marketing |
| `operator-admin-managed` | Operator/admin mutation via Web workspace UI or admin API |
| `system-automated` | Background job, outbox relay, cron, webhook handler |
| `internal-infrastructure` | Build, guard, codegen, dev tooling — usually out of feature scope |

### 2.1 Capability record (required fields)

```json
{
  "capabilityId": "CAP-001",
  "title": "Operator can refund a ticket",
  "class": "operator-admin-managed",
  "actor": "operator with finance.refund permission",
  "entryPoint": "Web > Tours > Ticket detail > Refund action",
  "permission": "finance.refund",
  "input": "ticketId, amount, reason",
  "stateTransition": "ticket.status: paid → refunded",
  "successState": "Refund confirmed; member notified",
  "loadingState": "Spinner on confirm; disable double-submit",
  "emptyState": "N/A — action on existing ticket",
  "errorState": "Inline error; toast on network failure",
  "deniedState": "403 with permission message",
  "auditRequirement": "audit log row with actor, tenantId, ticketId",
  "testEvidence": "ledger row for postgres spec + unit test",
  "browserEvidence": "screenshot desktop + mobile when user-visible"
}
```

### 2.2 Capability status (never conflate)

| Status | Meaning |
| ------ | ------- |
| `implemented` | Code exists for the capability path |
| `tested` | Automated test passed with ledger row |
| `browser-verified` | Real browser interaction with artifact |
| `production-verified` | Verified on production/staging with explicit unlock |
| `accepted-risk` | Documented gap with architect/user acceptance |
| `blocked` | Recoverable or hard-stop blocker open |
| `unverified` | Could not run required check (env, browser, etc.) |

**Forbidden completions:**

- Route, seed, stub, placeholder, read-only catalog, dispatcher-only, or fixture-only behavior → **not** a complete usable feature.
- Admin feature **not complete** unless authorized admin can perform the intended **mutation** through UI/API with permission checks, confirmation, validation, audit evidence, and browser proof when applicable.
- Passing build or curl alone → **not** browser-verified.

Store inventory in `.cache/feature-delivery/<sessionId>/requirement-inventory.json`.

---

## 3. Initial product / architecture / UI-UX design gate (CP1)

**No source implementation** until CP1 design brief is recorded and internally consistent.

### 3.1 Design brief (required sections)

1. Problem and user outcome
2. Member journey
3. Operator/admin journey
4. System-automation journey
5. Domain boundary
6. Platform-vs-module-vs-workspace-plugin classification
7. Existing consumers and future consumers (≥2 when platform candidate)
8. Data model and tenant/RLS approach
9. API and BFF contract
10. UI placement and information hierarchy
11. Desktop and mobile behavior
12. Persian RTL and English LTR behavior
13. Loading, empty, error, denied, and long-content states
14. Verification matrix (maps to capability records)
15. Known risks and explicit exclusions

Store as `.cache/feature-delivery/<sessionId>/design-brief.json` and summarize in the CP1 plan for user/architect review.

### 3.2 Design gate exit criteria

- All capability records have class, actor, and states defined.
- UI placement justified against existing IA (no new tab/route without inspection).
- Verification matrix lists every capability with expected evidence type.
- architecture-reviewer invoked at CP1 when platform candidate or protected paths.

---

## 4. Future-consumer analysis

For notifications, inboxes, outbox, attachments, identity, permissions, wallet, engagement, routing, shared SDKs, or reusable UI:

1. Search **≥2 plausible consumers** in code and docs.
2. Inspect existing ports and dispatchers.
3. Compare similar implementations.
4. Detect duplicate tables, contracts, dedupe keys, or parallel UI models.
5. Re-run after first vertical slice (CP3) and before DB/shared-contract work (CP4).

Use [notification-case-study](notification-case-study.mdoc) as regression fixture — not the only example.

---

## 5. Internet research policy

Perform focused web research when the problem involves:

- Current library/framework behavior
- Official API or platform documentation
- Accessibility standards (WCAG, ARIA)
- Security/RLS recommendations
- Current browser behavior
- UX patterns where placement or interaction is uncertain
- Blocker whose cause is unclear from repository evidence
- Architectural pattern that may have changed

### 5.1 Research rules

| Rule | Behavior |
| ---- | -------- |
| Sources | Prefer official docs, standards, primary sources, maintained project docs |
| Snippets | Do not rely on search snippets alone — fetch and read |
| Record | URL, query/topic, date, supported decision, `fact` or `inference` |
| Override | Never copy external architecture blindly; never override repo constraints |
| Unavailable | Record limitation; continue with local evidence |
| Hard stops | Research **never** overrides security, tenant/RLS, migration rewrite, production, or unresolved product decisions |

### 5.2 research.json

**Location:** `.cache/feature-delivery/<sessionId>/research.json`

```json
{
  "entries": [
    {
      "id": "RES-001",
      "query": "Playwright trace on timeout",
      "url": "https://playwright.dev/docs/trace-viewer",
      "date": "2026-09-04",
      "supportedDecision": "Enable trace on first retry for CP5 browser failures",
      "conclusionKind": "fact",
      "checkpoint": "CP6"
    }
  ]
}
```

Link ledger rows: `gate_id=research.<id>`.

---

## 6. UI/UX review gate (CP5)

For any **visible** feature:

| Check | Requirement |
| ----- | ----------- |
| Placement | Inspect existing UI before new tab/route — dashboard, profile, account, operator workspace, existing tabs |
| IA | Compare member vs operator information architecture |
| Tokens | Use design tokens and repository primitives — no raw controls where primitives exist |
| i18n | RTL (Persian) and LTR (English); long labels; responsive layout |
| States | Loading, empty, error, permission-denied, long content |
| Browser | Real interaction when available — desktop and mobile screenshots for important changes |
| Accessibility | Run axe or equivalent when available |
| Evidence | Never mark UI complete from API/curl/build alone |

If browser verification cannot run → capability status `unverified` or `accepted-risk`. **Never** silently convert to PASS.

Artifacts: `/opt/cursor/artifacts/` (Cloud) with ledger rows `gate_id=browser.<capabilityId>`.

---

## 7. Queued feature execution

Maintain `.cache/feature-delivery/<sessionId>/requirement-queue.json`:

```json
{
  "items": [
    {
      "capabilityId": "CAP-001",
      "status": "completed",
      "checkpoint": "CP6"
    },
    {
      "capabilityId": "CAP-002",
      "status": "recoverable-blocker",
      "blockerId": "BR-CODE-001"
    },
    {
      "capabilityId": "CAP-003",
      "status": "waiting-for-decision",
      "stopId": "SC-ARCH-02"
    },
    {
      "capabilityId": "CAP-004",
      "status": "not-started"
    }
  ]
}
```

| Queue status | Behavior |
| ------------ | -------- |
| `completed` | Capability status meets matrix |
| `in-progress` | Active work item |
| `recoverable-blocker` | Run blocker recovery; continue other items |
| `waiting-for-decision` | Hard stop — human architecture/security/product |
| `not-started` | Queued |
| `unverified` | Missing browser/env evidence |

When one sub-feature is blocked, **continue independent work**, then return to blocked item. Final report lists **every** queue item and exact status.

